    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "CaaAFMgwngqz"
      },
      "source": [
        "This model achieved a test accuracy and an AUC of ~**98%**, representing a significant and undeniable improvement over the previous results. This outcome clearly demonstrates that enhancing the quality of the dataset, rather than arbitrarily scaling the complexity of the neural network, is the most effective strategy for boosting the model's overall performance. By removing ambiguous signals through stricter ToF cuts, we provided the algorithm with a much clearer ground truth, proving that a simple, lightweight architecture is more than sufficient for this classification task when supported by high quality data."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "Lz12VWpA7gFc"
      },
      "source": [
        "# **RandomForestClassifier**"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "uqSw8rYr79WG"
      },
      "source": [
        "Deep Learning architectures, such as CNNs, are extremely powerful but often computationally demanding and overly complex for straightforward classification tasks. An effective alternative is to approach this binary classification using different traditional ML algorithms. The Random Forest was selected, because it provides a simpler, faster, and highly stable model naturally resistant to overfitting. This model is trained on the purified dataset, since it was demonstrated that it delivers significantly better performance.\\\n",
        "While Grid Search optimization was employed for the neural networks, it was bypassed for the Random Forest. The baseline configuration naturally achieves an outstanding accuracy of 98%, rendering exhaustive hyperparameter tuning redundant. Furthermore, bypassing this step avoids the excessive computational time associated with running heavy ensemble algorithms on standard CPU environments.\n",
        "\n",
        "Finally, before proceeding with the training, the shape of the input data must be adjusted to match the mathematical requirements of the algorithm."
      ]
    },
